export function concurrency(add,cpp){
const native={native:true,flags:'-pthread',reason:'This lesson requires native C++20 thread and synchronization support.'};
add('thread-coordination','joined-lifetime','Join before borrowed worker state is destroyed',
'A worker capturing local references borrows objects owned by its caller. The caller must keep those objects alive until the worker finishes. jthread supplies RAII joining, and an explicit join can establish completion before a result is observed.\n\nDestruction order matters: declare borrowed state before the thread owner so reverse destruction releases the thread first.',
cpp('iostream thread',"    int result = 0;\n    std::jthread worker([&] { result = 42; });\n    worker.join();\n    std::cout << \"joined=\" << result << \"\\n\";"),'joined=42',
'Joining establishes worker completion before main reads the result. The result’s lifetime covers the worker access.',
'Detaching a thread does not extend the lifetime of captured references.',
['What makes the result read synchronized?','The successful join','The integer being small','The lambda syntax'],'Thread completion synchronizes with the completed join.',
'Move the worker into an inner scope and rely on jthread destruction. Explain the required declaration and destruction order.',native);
add('thread-coordination','multi-lock','Acquire several mutexes with a consistent protocol',
'A compound operation can need locks for two resources. Opposite manual acquisition orders can deadlock. scoped_lock can acquire several mutexes with deadlock-avoidance behavior and release them with RAII.\n\nEvery conflicting access must follow a compatible locking protocol; locking only the transfer writer does not protect an unlocked reader.',
cpp('iostream mutex thread',"    int a = 10, b = 20;\n    std::mutex first, second;\n    auto transfer = [&] {\n        std::scoped_lock lock(first, second);\n        --a;\n        ++b;\n    };\n    std::jthread one(transfer), two(transfer);\n    one.join();\n    two.join();\n    std::cout << a << \" \" << b << \" total=\" << a + b << \"\\n\";"),'8 22 total=30',
'Both transfers hold the two locks while preserving the combined balance. Joining precedes the final unlocked observation.',
'A lock-order convention is ineffective if a helper silently acquires the same resources in the opposite order.',
['Which state must be protected as one unit?','Both balances during the transfer','Only the output stream','Only the destination balance'],'The invariant spans both shared values.',
'Add a concurrent observer using the same lock set. Check the conserved total without reading either balance outside the protocol.',native);
const queue="class Queue {\n    std::mutex mutex_;\n    std::condition_variable changed_;\n    std::deque<int> values_;\n    bool closed_ = false;\n\n  public:\n    bool push(int value) {\n        std::unique_lock lock(mutex_);\n        changed_.wait(lock, [&] { return closed_ || values_.size() < 2; });\n        if (closed_)\n            return false;\n        values_.push_back(value);\n        lock.unlock();\n        changed_.notify_all();\n        return true;\n    }\n    std::optional<int> pop() {\n        std::unique_lock lock(mutex_);\n        changed_.wait(lock, [&] { return closed_ || !values_.empty(); });\n        if (values_.empty())\n            return std::nullopt;\n        int value = values_.front();\n        values_.pop_front();\n        lock.unlock();\n        changed_.notify_all();\n        return value;\n    }\n    void close() {\n        std::lock_guard lock(mutex_);\n        closed_ = true;\n        changed_.notify_all();\n    }\n};";
add('thread-coordination','condition-queue','Wait for a predicate, not just a notification',
'Condition-variable notifications are not stored permits and waits can wake spuriously. A waiter must check a shared predicate while holding the mutex. The predicate also needs a shutdown alternative so an empty closed queue can stop waiting.\n\nThis bounded queue has capacity two and transfers integer values by ownership into a deque.',
cpp('condition_variable deque iostream mutex optional thread',"    Queue queue;\n    int sum = 0;\n    std::jthread consumer([&] {\n        while (auto value = queue.pop())\n            sum += *value;\n    });\n    for (int value : {1, 2, 3, 4, 5})\n        queue.push(value);\n    queue.close();\n    consumer.join();\n    std::cout << \"sum=\" << sum << \"\\n\";",queue),'sum=15',
'The producer may wait for capacity while the consumer may wait for data. Both waits recheck their predicates under the same mutex.',
'Waiting once without a predicate can consume absent data after a spurious wakeup.',
['What makes a consumer wait finish?','Data is available or the queue is closed','Any unrelated notification alone','A fixed sleep duration'],'The mutex-protected predicate defines readiness.',
'Add two consumers and aggregate results without racing. Check every produced value is consumed exactly once.',native);
add('thread-coordination','draining-close','Define whether close drains or discards pending work',
'Queue shutdown is part of the observable API. This queue rejects pushes after close but lets consumers drain already accepted values. Only closed-and-empty returns no next item.\n\nA different cancellation policy might discard pending work, but it needs a different explicit contract and ownership rule for abandoned items.',
cpp('condition_variable deque iostream mutex optional thread',"    Queue queue;\n    queue.push(7);\n    queue.push(8);\n    queue.close();\n    std::cout << \"rejected=\" << !queue.push(9) << \" values=\";\n    while (auto value = queue.pop())\n        std::cout << *value;\n    std::cout << \" done\\n\";",queue),'rejected=1 values=78 done',
'The two accepted values remain available in FIFO order after close, and the new push is rejected.',
'A shutdown flag that wakes consumers but not blocked producers can leave a thread waiting forever.',
['When does pop return no value?','When the queue is closed and empty','As soon as close is called even with pending work','Whenever a notification is lost'],'The draining policy retains accepted work until consumed.',
'Test close on an empty queue and close while a producer is waiting for space. Ensure all participants can finish.',native);
add('thread-coordination','cooperative-stop','A stop request is a cooperative signal',
'A stop token communicates a request; it does not forcibly terminate a thread or release its resources. A worker must observe the token and return through its normal ownership and cleanup paths.\n\nBlocking operations need a corresponding stop-aware wakeup strategy. Merely setting a token does not make an ordinary condition-variable wait complete.',
cpp('atomic iostream stop_token thread',"    std::atomic<bool> observed{false};\n    std::jthread worker([&](std::stop_token token) {\n        while (!token.stop_requested())\n            std::this_thread::yield();\n        observed.store(true);\n    });\n    worker.request_stop();\n    worker.join();\n    std::cout << \"observed=\" << observed.load() << \"\\n\";"),'observed=1',
'The worker eventually observes the request and exits before join completes. The example does not print schedule-dependent spin counts.',
'A worker blocked forever in an unrelated operation may never reach its token check.',
['Does request_stop kill the worker immediately?','No, the worker cooperates','Yes','Only if the worker owns a mutex'],'The token is a signal, not forced termination.',
'Add a stop_callback that wakes a waiting protocol, or use a supported stop-aware wait. Test cleanup after a stop request.',native);

add('atomics-memory','atomic-rmw','An atomic read-modify-write prevents lost counter increments',
'An atomic fetch_add combines observation and update into one indivisible operation on that atomic object. Two separate load/store calls are not equivalent to a read-modify-write and can lose concurrent increments.\n\nThis counter needs atomicity but does not publish other data, so relaxed ordering is sufficient for the increments themselves. Joins establish completion before the final observation.',
cpp('atomic iostream thread',"    std::atomic<unsigned> count{0};\n    auto work = [&] {\n        for (unsigned i = 0; i < 10000; ++i)\n            count.fetch_add(1, std::memory_order_relaxed);\n    };\n    std::jthread first(work), second(work);\n    first.join();\n    second.join();\n    std::cout << \"count=\" << count.load() << \"\\n\";"),'count=20000',
'Every fetch_add contributes one increment, producing exactly twenty thousand after both workers join.',
'An atomic load followed by an atomic store is still two operations and can overwrite another thread’s increment.',
['What operation combines the update atomically?','fetch_add','load followed by store','A non-atomic ++ protected only by hope'],'The read-modify-write is indivisible for this atomic object.',
'Implement a deliberately separate load/store model under a controlled schedule to demonstrate a lost update without introducing a data race.',native);
add('atomics-memory','compare-exchange','A failed compare-exchange updates the expected value',
'compare_exchange compares an atomic object with an expected value and conditionally publishes a desired value. On failure, it writes the observed value back into expected. This supports retry loops without an extra uncoordinated assumption.\n\nWeak compare-exchange can also fail spuriously and is normally used in a loop. This fixture uses the strong operation for one deterministic mismatch.',
cpp('atomic iostream',"    std::atomic<int> value{7};\n    int expected = 5;\n    bool changed = value.compare_exchange_strong(expected, 9);\n    std::cout << \"changed=\" << changed << \" expected=\" << expected\n              << \" value=\" << value.load() << \"\\n\";"),'changed=0 expected=7 value=7',
'The initial expected value does not match, so the atomic remains seven and expected becomes seven.',
'A retry loop that never accounts for the updated expected state can apply the wrong decision rule.',
['What does expected contain after this failure?','The observed value seven','The desired value nine','Its original value five'],'Failure reports the currently observed atomic value through expected.',
'Retry with a decision based on the observed value. Specify whether the desired update should be recomputed after each failure.',native);
add('atomics-memory','release-acquire-data','Publish a prepared batch before readers inspect it',
'A release store can publish preceding initialization to a reader that observes it through an acquire load. The ordering relation applies to the protected prior writes; it does not permit future unsynchronized mutation of that same data.\n\nKeep the published object alive through reader completion and make the single-publication limitation explicit.',
cpp('array atomic iostream thread',"    std::array<int, 2> values{};\n    std::atomic<bool> ready{false};\n    int result = 0;\n    std::jthread reader([&] {\n        while (!ready.load(std::memory_order_acquire))\n            std::this_thread::yield();\n        result = values[0] + values[1];\n    });\n    values = {20, 22};\n    ready.store(true, std::memory_order_release);\n    reader.join();\n    std::cout << \"batch=\" << result << \"\\n\";"),'batch=42',
'The reader observes the release and then reads the initialized pair. The writer performs no later mutation.',
'Changing the flag operations to relaxed removes this publication guarantee for the non-atomic array.',
['What is made visible by observing the release?','The preceding array initialization','Every future mutation automatically','The compiler’s temporary files'],'Acquire/release establishes visibility of the writes ordered before publication.',
'Draw the happens-before chain explicitly. Explain why publishing a pointer without retaining its owner would still be unsafe.',native);
add('atomics-memory','sequential-consistency','Sequential consistency constrains the global atomic order',
'Sequentially consistent atomic operations participate in a single total order compatible with each thread’s sequencing requirements. In the classic two-store/two-load fixture, both reads cannot observe zero under that model.\n\nThe exact observed pair is schedule-dependent, so the program checks an allowed-outcome property rather than asserting one arbitrary interleaving.',
cpp('atomic iostream thread',"    std::atomic<int> a{0}, b{0};\n    int first = 0, second = 0;\n    std::jthread one([&] {\n        a.store(1);\n        first = b.load();\n    });\n    std::jthread two([&] {\n        b.store(1);\n        second = a.load();\n    });\n    one.join();\n    two.join();\n    bool allowed = !(first == 0 && second == 0);\n    std::cout << \"allowed=\" << allowed << \"\\n\";\n    return allowed ? 0 : 1;"),'allowed=1',
'The check permits all outcomes consistent with the stated model and rejects the forbidden both-zero result.',
'Printing one observed schedule repeatedly does not prove that other permitted schedules cannot occur.',
['Why does the output avoid one exact read pair?','Several schedules are permitted','Atomic loads return random values','The test ignores all results'],'The contract constrains outcomes without fixing the scheduler.',
'Model the ordering constraints on paper. Compare with relaxed operations conceptually without claiming a finite run proves a memory-model theorem.',native);
add('atomics-memory','atomic-snapshot-owner','Publish an owning immutable snapshot handle',
'C++20 supports atomic shared_ptr operations that combine publication with owning handle transfer. A reader holding an old shared owner can continue reading that immutable version after a newer one is published.\n\nThe pointed-to data must remain immutable through that read contract. Atomic handle access does not make arbitrary mutation of the pointee safe, and lock-free implementation is not guaranteed.',
cpp('atomic iostream memory vector',"    using Snapshot = std::shared_ptr<const std::vector<int>>;\n    std::atomic<Snapshot> current{\n        std::make_shared<const std::vector<int>>(std::initializer_list<int>{1, 2})};\n    auto old = current.load();\n    current.store(\n        std::make_shared<const std::vector<int>>(std::initializer_list<int>{3, 4}));\n    std::cout << old->at(0) << \" \" << current.load()->at(0) << \"\\n\";"),'1 3',
'The retained old owner still sees its original vector while a new load obtains the replacement snapshot.',
'Atomic shared_ptr does not promise lock-free behavior on every implementation.',
['Why is the old snapshot still alive?','The reader retains a shared owner','The atomic permanently keeps every version','Its vector capacity is small'],'Ownership, not just publication ordering, protects its lifetime.',
'Hold several readers across replacement and observe reclamation through weak_ptr expiration after their owners are released.',native);

const store="class Store {\n    mutable std::mutex mutex_;\n    unsigned revision_ = 0;\n    std::map<unsigned, int> rows_{{1, 10}};\n\n  public:\n    std::pair<unsigned, int> read(unsigned id) const {\n        std::lock_guard lock(mutex_);\n        return {revision_, rows_.at(id)};\n    }\n    bool update(unsigned id, int value, unsigned expected) {\n        std::lock_guard lock(mutex_);\n        if (revision_ != expected)\n            return false;\n        rows_.at(id) = value;\n        ++revision_;\n        return true;\n    }\n};";
add('concurrent-storage','locked-store','Return a value copy from a locked read',
'A lock protects shared map access only while held. Returning a reference to a mutable row and releasing the lock can expose later unsynchronized access. An owning value copy lets the caller inspect a stable result after the lock is released.\n\nThis store copies a revision and one scalar value under the same mutex so the pair describes one observation.',
cpp('iostream map mutex utility',"    Store store;\n    auto [revision, value] = store.read(1);\n    std::cout << \"revision=\" << revision << \" value=\" << value << \"\\n\";",store),'revision=0 value=10',
'The returned pair owns its scalar values and does not borrow the protected map element.',
'Returning const Row& does not keep a mutex held or prevent another owner from mutating the row.',
['Why return a value from this read?','It remains independent after unlocking','It keeps the map locked forever','It prevents every future write'],'The copied observation has no borrowed mutable storage.',
'Extend the row to own a string and return a complete Row copy. Compare the lifetime contract with a scoped locked-view API.',native);
add('concurrent-storage','revision-under-lock','Validate and update under the same mutex',
'Optimistic updates carry the revision associated with a prior observation. Validation and publication must occur under one synchronization boundary so another writer cannot slip between the check and the write.\n\nThis whole-store revision protocol is coarse: even unrelated row updates can invalidate an observation. That limitation should be documented rather than hidden.',
cpp('iostream map mutex utility',"    Store store;\n    auto first = store.read(1), stale = first;\n    bool accepted = store.update(1, 20, first.first);\n    bool rejected = !store.update(1, 30, stale.first);\n    std::cout << accepted << \" \" << rejected << \" value=\" << store.read(1).second\n              << \"\\n\";",store),'1 1 value=20',
'The first update advances the revision; the stale second update is rejected while preserving twenty.',
'An unlocked revision check followed by a locked write can still overwrite newer state.',
['Which operations share the lock?','Revision comparison and mutation','Only the mutation after an unlocked check','Only the prior read'],'The comparison must stay valid until the update publishes.',
'Coordinate two worker threads so both capture one revision before either updates. Check one success and one stale rejection.',native);
add('concurrent-storage','reader-writer-lock','Shared reading needs a consistent writer protocol',
'shared_mutex permits several shared holders while excluding an exclusive writer. Every access to the protected state must still use the same protocol; a shared lock is not permission to mutate ordinary members.\n\nThe fixture checks reader observations and performs the write after joining readers to keep the expected result deterministic.',
cpp('iostream mutex shared_mutex thread',"    std::shared_mutex mutex;\n    int value = 7, first = 0, second = 0;\n    std::jthread one([&] {\n        std::shared_lock lock(mutex);\n        first = value;\n    });\n    std::jthread two([&] {\n        std::shared_lock lock(mutex);\n        second = value;\n    });\n    one.join();\n    two.join();\n    {\n        std::unique_lock lock(mutex);\n        value = 9;\n    }\n    std::cout << first << \" \" << second << \" final=\" << value << \"\\n\";"),'7 7 final=9',
'Both readers observe the initial value under shared locks; the later exclusive operation changes it to nine.',
'A shared lock does not automatically make mutation through another alias legal.',
['Which mode should protect a write?','Exclusive ownership of the mutex','Shared ownership','No lock if readers are fast'],'Writers must exclude conflicting readers and writers.',
'Add a coordinated overlapping writer and define allowed reader outcomes. Avoid assuming fairness or a particular scheduling order.',native);
add('concurrent-storage','compound-storage-invariant','Protect multi-field consistency as one observation',
'A storage invariant can span a row count, an index entry, and a page state. Protecting each field independently does not automatically make a reader’s combined observation consistent.\n\nThis bounded fixture uses one lock around a two-field transfer and one lock around the total observation. The point is the invariant boundary, not a claim that one global lock scales indefinitely.',
cpp('iostream mutex thread',"    int free = 8, used = 2;\n    std::mutex mutex;\n    auto allocate = [&] {\n        std::lock_guard lock(mutex);\n        if (free) {\n            --free;\n            ++used;\n        }\n    };\n    std::jthread one(allocate), two(allocate);\n    one.join();\n    two.join();\n    std::lock_guard lock(mutex);\n    std::cout << \"free=\" << free << \" used=\" << used << \" total=\" << free + used\n              << \"\\n\";"),'free=6 used=4 total=10',
'Both allocation transitions preserve the total capacity of ten, and the final observation covers the pair together.',
'Two atomic counters can individually be race-free while an unlocked reader observes an impossible combined state.',
['What defines the required synchronization boundary?','The compound invariant','The number of source lines','The size of each integer'],'Related fields must be updated and observed coherently.',
'Replace the counters with a row map and index map. Define and test which lock scope preserves cross-structure consistency.',native);
add('concurrent-storage','snapshot-reclamation','Reclaim old data only after readers release ownership',
'Publishing a replacement does not imply that old readers have finished. Snapshot ownership can retain an old immutable value until the last reader releases it. A weak observer can expose when reclamation becomes possible without owning the old version itself.\n\nThis is a lifetime model; production page reclamation also needs generation IDs, synchronized reader registration, and resource accounting.',
cpp('iostream memory',"    auto current = std::make_shared<const int>(10);\n    auto reader = current;\n    std::weak_ptr<const int> old = current;\n    current = std::make_shared<const int>(20);\n    std::cout << \"retained=\" << !old.expired() << \" \";\n    reader.reset();\n    std::cout << \"released=\" << old.expired() << \"\\n\";"),'retained=1 released=1',
'Replacing current removes one owner, but reader still retains the old value until reset.',
'Reusing old page storage immediately after publication can invalidate a reader that still references the old generation.',
['When can the old value be reclaimed?','After its last owning reader releases it','Immediately after current changes','When the next write starts'],'Publication and reclamation are distinct events.',
'Model two readers with different completion times. Confirm the old generation remains retained until both release it.',native);
}
